{"attack": true,"case": "The target's central mechanism relies on the analogy that preferring corroborated theories is a constitutive norm 'similar to check your king in chess.' This analogy is self-undermining. Constitutive rules of games are arbitrary conventions: they define the activity but have no justification outside the activity itself—there is no reason chess requires checking the king rather than some other rule. If the rule 'prefer the better-corroborated theory' is constitutive in exactly this sense, then it is an arbitrary convention with no rational defense: one could with equal constitutive legitimacy adopt 'prefer the theory of the eldest scientist.' The only thing that distinguishes the corroboration rule as worth adopting over arbitrary alternatives is that it tracks truth or reliability—which is an epistemic justification that, per Popper's problem, must not smuggle in induction. Thus the chess analogy either makes the rule arbitrary (so not 'rationally defensible,' merely conventional) or forces an appeal to truth-conduciveness (reintroducing the very inductive assumption the target sought to avoid). The target's own mechanistic framing collapses its claim of defensibility.","premise": "A constitutive rule of a game is an arbitrary convention; if the corroboration-preference rule is constitutive in that sense, it lacks rational defense, and if it is not arbitrary, it needs a truth-tracking justification that reintroduces induction.","premise_evidence": [{"block": "ab0e433b88f01da72de17512cac3dc05cd34992155da937ba65f9578f8f7c6d0","quote": "The rule 'prefer the better-corroborated theory' is a constitutive norm of this institution, similar to 'check your king' in chess. It does not require individual scientists to believe in induction; it requires them to follow the rule to participate in the game."}],"counterexample": ["Consider the constitutive rule 'prefer the theory proposed by the scientist whose surname is alphabetically first.' Under the target's chess-analogy logic, this is an equally valid constitutive norm of a hypothetical scientific institution. If both rules are merely constitutive conventions, the target provides no non-arbitrary reason to adopt the corroboration rule over the alphabetical one—yet the target claims the corroboration preference is 'defensible.' Defensibility requires a justification that distinguishes it, which the constitutive-convention framework cannot supply without appealing to truth-tracking, i.e., induction."],"successor_question": "Can a constitutive norm of a truth-aimed institution be rationally defensible without appeal to the truth-tracking properties that justify it over arbitrary alternatives?"}